-- title:
--   Adjoining a p-th root of an S-unit keeps unramifiedness outside S
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a finite set of rational primes, let $p$ be a prime with $(p)\in S$, and let $F$ be an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}=\mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\,\mathbb Q$ over $\mathbb Q$ which is unramified outside $S$ in the sense of the project predicate [`IntermediateField.IsUnramifiedOutside`](def/GroupCohomology_ContinuousUnramified.html#L16): $F$ is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb Q$ and, for every prime $q\notin S$ and every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $q$ (meaning $q\in A.\mathrm{nonunits}$), the subgroup $A.\mathrm{inertiaSubgroupIn}\ \mathbb Q$ of $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ — the image under the inclusion of the decomposition subgroup of the inertia subgroup of $A$ — is contained in the pointwise fixing subgroup of $F$. Let $\alpha\in\overline{\mathbb Q}$ satisfy $\alpha^p\in F$, and assume that $\alpha^p$ is a unit at every valuation subring away from $S$: for each valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ such that no $q\in S$ lies under $A$, both $\alpha^p\in A$ and $(\alpha^p)^{-1}\in A$. The conclusion is that the compositum $F\sqcup\mathbb Q(\alpha)$, the join of $F$ with the intermediate field generated by $\alpha$, is again finite over $\mathbb Q$ and unramified outside $S$ in the same sense.
--
--   This is the standard Kummer-theoretic ramification statement: adjoining a $p$-th root of an $S$-unit of $F$ introduces no ramification outside $S$, provided $p\in S$. It is used in the construction of auxiliary finite extensions unramified outside a fixed finite set of primes, and is cited in the level-arithmetic and continuous cohomology parts of the development, for instance in producing extensions unramified outside $S$ with prescribed decomposition behaviour and in the vanishing arguments for the $S$-units representation.
